WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.830
Parkfield, California, sits
on the San Andreas fault.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.690
From 1857, it had six
earthquakes of about magnitude 6.

3
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.030
The last struck in 1966.

4
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.750
In 1984, scientists began a
prediction experiment there,

5
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.930
and waited for the next one.

6
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.350
In Chile, GPS had shown a stretch
of plate boundary was locked.

7
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.170
In 2010, a magnitude 8.8
earthquake struck there.

8
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.490
Scientists flagged both places.

9
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.130
Why couldn't they say when?

10
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.270
In this mission, you'll
test seismic gap theory:

11
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.570
the idea that long-quiet segments are where
the next big earthquake is most likely.

12
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.630
Then you'll weigh dilatancy, a search
for warning signs in cracking rock.

13
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.270
Your mission: evaluate how far scientists
can say where a big earthquake will

14
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.040
strike, and why they cannot say when.
